00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:03.020
Hello this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:06.700
You credit card number probably looks pretty random to you.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.060
But hidden inside those mathematical digits, is a secret mathematical code.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.500
In this video, I'll teach you a magic trick based on this pattern

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.700
You'll need a credit card, a debit card, or an ATM card that has 16 digits.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.600
You'll also want a pencil and paper to do simple calculations.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:36.200
For this video, I'm going to do the calculation for the number that's written on the screen, but you should follow along with your credit card number.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:44.340
To get started, focus on the sixteen digits in the number, and separate them into two groups.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:52.180
For the first step, add up every other digit. In this case, the digits which are colored in black.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:01:01.780
We'll add up 1, 3, 5, 7 ,8, 0, 0, 0, to get to a sum of 24.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.520
We'll go ahead and double this result.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.000
24*2 is 48

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.780
Now look at the numbers which you just added up

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.680
Count the number of digits which are 5 or larger

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:30.720
In this case, the numbers five, seven, and eight, are five or larger, so that means that there are three numbers that are five and larger

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.860
Finally, add up the remaining digits.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.080
These are the digits that are colored in blue.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.660
2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + 9 + 0 + 0 + 0

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.040
This gives a sum of 29

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.500
Now we're ready to do the magic part

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.320
We have three numbers, 48, 3 , and 29.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:05.420
When you add up these three numbers, we're going to end up with a number, who's last digit is 0

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.300
And this is true, for every single credit card number

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:19.200
So that's the magic part, you can have someone do this, they'll do these calculations and then you are going to say" Alright look at your number at the last digit is zero"

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.460
You might be wondering why this works.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:28.060
This, in fact, is a mathematical code, that is programmed into your credit card number.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:42.740
The reason it's in there is because, if you every typed your credit card number into a website and you accidentaly typed the wrong number, the website doesn't know, that it needs to know how to validate a correct number from a incorrect number.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.960
So this is one way that they check that you've typed in a credit card number that's valid.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:55.740
If you end up with a sum whose last digit is not zero, then that means that you probably typed in your credit card number incorrectly

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:10.060
So that's a pretty neat magic trick and someone who doesn't know that this is encoded in the magi~ in the credit card number, is going to be surprised you knew the last digit of this number is zero

